WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.615 --> 00:00:04.709
- You see the kids buggin'
you, he puts his hands on you,

00:00:04.709 --> 00:00:07.622
you pick up the nearest rock

00:00:07.622 --> 00:00:09.945
or whatever you can get your
hands and you lay him out.

00:00:09.945 --> 00:00:11.446
- So you consider yourself lucky.

00:00:11.446 --> 00:00:12.589
- Very lucky.

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:15.108
- But you have to have talent
to meet the luck, right?

00:00:15.108 --> 00:00:18.014
- Somebody hands you the ball and you run.

00:00:18.014 --> 00:00:20.237
So ambition was a dirty
word to me because it felt

00:00:20.237 --> 00:00:24.726
like you were working for
some gain or material...

00:00:24.726 --> 00:00:26.067
- He's widely regarded as one of

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:28.307
the world's biggest film stars.

00:00:28.307 --> 00:00:32.132
He rose to prominence in his
role in a 1980's tv series,

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:35.837
21 Jump Street where he
quickly became a teen idol.

00:00:35.837 --> 00:00:38.799
He was listed in the 2012
Guinness Book of World Records

00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:43.641
as the highest paid actor
earning over 75 million dollars.

00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:45.367
He's Johnny Depp and here's my take

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.545
on his top ten rules of success.

00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:49.235
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:49.235 --> 00:00:50.920
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.368
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:52.368 --> 00:00:54.061
And you guys know the
drill, if you hear something

00:00:54.061 --> 00:00:57.263
that Johnny says that really
inspires you please leave it in

00:00:57.263 --> 00:00:59.163
the comments below and
put quotes around it

00:00:59.163 --> 00:01:01.249
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:02.082
Enjoy.

00:01:05.578 --> 00:01:07.911
(pop music)

00:01:15.696 --> 00:01:19.863
- If we could celebrate
every breath that we take,

00:01:23.687 --> 00:01:26.261
because nothing's a guarantee.

00:01:26.261 --> 00:01:30.094
If we can celebrate the
breath and the exhale,

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:35.000
which is, being alive, without you know,

00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:40.675
"Oh my car won't start.
Oh geeze, you know,"

00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:43.036
"the cat dropped dead."

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:46.595
(audience laughs)

00:01:46.595 --> 00:01:48.928
"I think I need a lobotomy."

00:01:52.102 --> 00:01:54.303
I mean, there are things
that are very very important

00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:59.171
in life that we, we just, we
don't think about, you know?

00:01:59.171 --> 00:02:02.727
And one of the most worrisome
things that I went through

00:02:02.727 --> 00:02:06.560
for years, you're freaked
out about your past,

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.354
you're worried, you're scared
to death of the future,

00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:13.910
and that's all you're thinking about.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.741
And the now doesn't exist.

00:02:15.741 --> 00:02:16.883
You know?

00:02:16.883 --> 00:02:19.872
And I think that's a grave
mistake that we make.

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:21.321
One of the greatest pieces of advice

00:02:21.321 --> 00:02:23.294
I've ever gotten in my
life was from my mom,

00:02:23.294 --> 00:02:25.545
you know, when I was a little kid.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.453
There was a kid that was
bugging me, you know, in school.

00:02:28.453 --> 00:02:31.540
She said, "Okay, I'm going
to tell you what to do."

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.859
She said, "The kid's bugging
you, he puts his hands on you,"

00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:39.434
"you pick up the nearest
rock, or whatever you can get"

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:42.517
"your hands on, and you lay him out."

00:02:47.449 --> 00:02:48.282
And I did.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.067
And I felt better.

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:53.234
And it worked.

00:02:55.944 --> 00:02:59.574
So, for me, you know,
that was a great lesson,

00:02:59.574 --> 00:03:03.741
cause someone had invaded
my space, invaded my being,

00:03:06.327 --> 00:03:10.944
you know, and doing something
against me that I didn't

00:03:10.944 --> 00:03:15.504
want to be done, so I took
control of the situation,

00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:17.620
and I ran with it.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:21.703
And, um, many years later,
after being, you know,

00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:28.684
turned into a product, you know,
by a very huge corporation,

00:03:30.623 --> 00:03:32.763
that had their hands all over me,

00:03:32.763 --> 00:03:35.680
and I couldn't escape it, you know.

00:03:38.528 --> 00:03:43.038
I promised myself that I would
continue to move forward,

00:03:43.038 --> 00:03:47.205
and do my best not to compromise
in any way whatsoever,

00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:52.622
you know, not allow anyone
to put their hands on me,

00:03:52.622 --> 00:03:53.872
and affect me in that way.

00:03:53.872 --> 00:03:57.221
So, that's the best
advice I think I can give,

00:03:57.221 --> 00:03:58.670
is just to keep moving forward,

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.933
and don't give a shit
about what anybody thinks,

00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:03.842
just keep moving forward,
and do what you have to do,

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:04.688
for you.

00:04:04.688 --> 00:04:07.757
- Paul Newman told me that
any successful person,

00:04:07.757 --> 00:04:11.761
in any field, who in
discussing their career,

00:04:11.761 --> 00:04:15.011
doesn't use the word "luck" is a liar.

00:04:15.011 --> 00:04:17.594
- Oh, yeah, he's absolutely right, yeah.

00:04:17.594 --> 00:04:19.158
- So you consider yourself lucky.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.591
- Very lucky, yeah.

00:04:20.591 --> 00:04:22.877
- But you have to have talent
to meet the luck, right?

00:04:22.877 --> 00:04:27.223
- If somebody hands you the
ball, and you run, you know,

00:04:27.223 --> 00:04:28.798
and if you get hit, you get hit.

00:04:28.798 --> 00:04:31.624
Or maybe you make it
through, you never know.

00:04:31.624 --> 00:04:34.396
But I just know that
somebody handed me the ball

00:04:34.396 --> 00:04:36.023
at a certain point,

00:04:36.023 --> 00:04:38.884
and I was hungry enough to keep running.

00:04:38.884 --> 00:04:40.296
And I'm still running.

00:04:40.296 --> 00:04:43.879
I maintain the hunger,
but not an ambition.

00:04:45.246 --> 00:04:49.413
You know, I'm very happy to
explore all possibilities

00:04:50.887 --> 00:04:54.804
of a character, and really
dive in to the role,

00:04:58.638 --> 00:05:02.297
I mean, to the point of where
Disney wanted to fire me.

00:05:02.297 --> 00:05:04.015
- They wanted to fire you from Pirates?

00:05:04.015 --> 00:05:04.848
- Yeah.

00:05:04.848 --> 00:05:05.977
- Because?

00:05:05.977 --> 00:05:08.028
- They couldn't understand
what I was doing.

00:05:08.028 --> 00:05:09.567
They didn't understand the character.

00:05:09.567 --> 00:05:12.987
They were actually contemplating
subtitling the film,

00:05:12.987 --> 00:05:13.820
you know.

00:05:15.303 --> 00:05:17.710
- And you went to play
music when you quit school,

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:19.069
you didn't go to become an actor.

00:05:19.069 --> 00:05:22.920
- I had already started playing
the guitar at the age of 12,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.087
I felt a passion for, yeah,
it was the first real passion

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:31.746
that I had.

00:05:32.845 --> 00:05:37.245
And I can safely say because
I felt then that the word

00:05:37.245 --> 00:05:40.245
ambition has become dirty. You know?

00:05:41.215 --> 00:05:42.048
- Yeah.

00:05:42.048 --> 00:05:45.614
- Because ambition somehow
implies that there is..

00:05:45.614 --> 00:05:46.605
(baby cries)

00:05:46.605 --> 00:05:47.438
I know.

00:05:50.086 --> 00:05:51.753
I know, I know. Cry.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:58.517
I'll probably be crying
in about 10 minutes.

00:05:59.585 --> 00:06:01.160
So ambition's a dirty word to me,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.778
because it felt like you
were working for some gain,

00:06:04.778 --> 00:06:07.640
or some material, or some status, or,

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:11.807
so I literally don't remember
going through puberty,

00:06:14.693 --> 00:06:17.554
because I locked myself in a
room at the age of 12, 12-13,

00:06:17.554 --> 00:06:21.552
and just played the guitar,
and learned things off records,

00:06:21.552 --> 00:06:23.333
and taught myself how to play.

00:06:23.333 --> 00:06:24.916
And it was my life.

00:06:26.257 --> 00:06:28.650
And it was the only solace,

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.259
it was the only sanctuary,
it was the only security,

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:34.009
it was my first love.

00:06:35.112 --> 00:06:36.515
- At the end of our session yesterday,

00:06:36.515 --> 00:06:40.967
you said, "Thank you to Scott
for reviving my career,"

00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:41.992
- Yeah.

00:06:41.992 --> 00:06:43.571
- And I don't know if that's in jest,

00:06:43.571 --> 00:06:46.324
because I've never had
the impression that you,

00:06:46.324 --> 00:06:48.923
you're desperate for Box Office.

00:06:48.923 --> 00:06:50.673
- I'm begging for it.

00:06:51.562 --> 00:06:55.729
It's been the thrust of
my interest since day one,

00:06:57.464 --> 00:06:59.209
since I was about 19.

00:06:59.209 --> 00:07:00.441
Yeah, that's all I care about.

00:07:00.441 --> 00:07:02.762
20 years of failures will do that to you.

00:07:02.762 --> 00:07:07.406
- But, on a different
level, why do you say that,

00:07:07.406 --> 00:07:10.402
and how do you feel when you
choose a role that ends up

00:07:10.402 --> 00:07:13.819
satisfying you that this one clearly did.

00:07:14.765 --> 00:07:18.085
- Well, you know, for me, with
every character that you play

00:07:18.085 --> 00:07:22.252
as long as you've reached that,
you've arrived at that place

00:07:24.808 --> 00:07:28.975
where you believe is the right
place to be for the character

00:07:31.221 --> 00:07:35.388
and if you feel you've done
service to the director,

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:39.229
to yourself, to the author,

00:07:39.229 --> 00:07:41.646
then to me, that's a success.

00:07:43.575 --> 00:07:47.455
Box Office is kind of,
that's something else,

00:07:47.455 --> 00:07:49.488
that's certainly none of my business.

00:07:49.488 --> 00:07:52.610
Once I'm wrapped on a film,
it's just to say good bye

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:54.949
to the character, and then move forward.

00:07:54.949 --> 00:07:57.616
How ironic is it that, you know,

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:00.549
- You were pumping gas.

00:08:00.549 --> 00:08:02.464
- I was pumping gas, most definitely.

00:08:02.464 --> 00:08:04.779
Printing T-shirts, and selling
ink pens, and you know,

00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:06.779
anything and everything.

00:08:07.935 --> 00:08:10.824
Yeah, and then the fact that
you have a 20 year career

00:08:10.824 --> 00:08:13.022
of failures, and then
you do a Pirate movie,

00:08:13.022 --> 00:08:14.426
and that buys you an island,

00:08:14.426 --> 00:08:16.926
the irony of that is pretty...

00:08:17.988 --> 00:08:20.292
- Do you think about the times

00:08:20.292 --> 00:08:23.512
when things weren't so good a lot?

00:08:23.512 --> 00:08:27.937
- Yeah, yeah. But I, you know,
there was a guy that I worked

00:08:27.937 --> 00:08:29.856
with many years ago, and
we were talking about

00:08:29.856 --> 00:08:32.042
success and money, and all that stuff,

00:08:32.042 --> 00:08:34.283
and he told me this one thing, he said,

00:08:34.283 --> 00:08:38.450
"Money doesn't change
anybody. Money reveals them."

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:44.937
Same thing with success. And
I believe that wholeheartedly,

00:08:46.683 --> 00:08:50.499
I think I've been revealed, I
don't think I haven't changed,

00:08:50.499 --> 00:08:55.159
I'm still exactly the same
guy that used to pump gas.

00:08:55.159 --> 00:08:58.542
I'm still the guy that was
a mechanic for a minute.

00:08:58.542 --> 00:09:02.357
I just happen to have a
weirder job at the moment.

00:09:02.357 --> 00:09:06.045
When I was approaching the
character of Willy Wonka,

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:09.519
you know, we all grew up
with the Gene Wilder version,

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:12.436
and it's fantastic. It's fantastic.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:20.897
But you know, I mean,
why go in that direction.

00:09:22.002 --> 00:09:24.953
It's been done. Why repeat. Why?

00:09:24.953 --> 00:09:29.455
So I found my own version.
And, my version was,

00:09:29.455 --> 00:09:33.288
I kept thinking of
children's television hosts

00:09:36.947 --> 00:09:38.197
that scared me.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:45.793
Because they're unpredictable,
and they have that

00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:47.876
very everything was okay.

00:09:52.443 --> 00:09:54.703
And it wasn't okay.

00:09:54.703 --> 00:09:58.870
But so, I used these children
television hosts sort of ideas

00:10:02.352 --> 00:10:06.400
and then the other ingredient
that I thought was what really

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.483
found the character, I
thought of George W. Bush.

00:10:12.025 --> 00:10:14.858
(audience laughs)

00:10:18.032 --> 00:10:20.449
If he were incredibly stoned.

00:10:24.762 --> 00:10:28.012
But like not just uncomfortably stoned.

00:10:30.599 --> 00:10:34.766
Past what could have been the
uncomfortable paranoia stage

00:10:37.066 --> 00:10:41.233
where he just sort of doesn't
know what he's saying.

00:10:44.363 --> 00:10:47.206
- Okay, Mad Hatter. Who
is you're Mad Hatter?

00:10:47.206 --> 00:10:51.039
- Um, the Mad Hatter was
basically a kind of a

00:11:00.379 --> 00:11:02.073
this is real weird.

00:11:02.073 --> 00:11:03.990
So my kids had a tutor.

00:11:05.603 --> 00:11:09.771
We were on the road, working
you know, so we had this tutor,

00:11:09.771 --> 00:11:13.938
her name's Katherine. She's
one of those English people,

00:11:15.058 --> 00:11:18.808
very proper, but Katherine
would talk to you,

00:11:20.609 --> 00:11:24.359
she had a little bit of
a thing in her voice,

00:11:27.689 --> 00:11:31.737
so I sponged her up as much as I could,

00:11:31.737 --> 00:11:35.267
because what I loved
about her was she would

00:11:35.267 --> 00:11:38.600
sort of talk to you like this, you know,

00:11:41.572 --> 00:11:42.771
some people even avert their eyes,

00:11:42.771 --> 00:11:47.765
they actually close their eyes
when they're looking at you,

00:11:47.765 --> 00:11:50.932
right? You guys have felt that before?

00:11:51.885 --> 00:11:52.802
It's weird.

00:11:54.591 --> 00:11:58.758
So, I used Katherine, because
when she closed her eyes,

00:11:59.938 --> 00:12:01.890
she's looking at you,
and let you know it was

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.914
very, very important.

00:12:03.914 --> 00:12:06.747
(audience laughs)

00:12:14.201 --> 00:12:16.478
She'll probably find this out
tomorrow and there will be

00:12:16.478 --> 00:12:17.468
a lawsuit.

00:12:17.468 --> 00:12:20.866
There were other things that
I probably should have done,

00:12:20.866 --> 00:12:24.225
not from my perspective, I
mean from my point of view

00:12:24.225 --> 00:12:25.503
I did the right things.

00:12:25.503 --> 00:12:29.670
Every film that I've done, I'm
happy that I made that choice

00:12:31.215 --> 00:12:33.510
I don't have any regrets, whatsoever.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:37.260
But I mean, in terms of
sustaining Box Office

00:12:43.695 --> 00:12:46.981
bank ability, I should have
done a few other things

00:12:46.981 --> 00:12:50.981
that I didn't do, but I'm
glad I didn't do them.

00:12:51.868 --> 00:12:53.888
- Because it wouldn't have been you.

00:12:53.888 --> 00:12:57.259
- Yeah, but it would have
been for the wrong reason.

00:12:57.259 --> 00:13:00.127
Everything that I've
been lucky enough to do,

00:13:00.127 --> 00:13:01.618
I did for the right reason.

00:13:01.618 --> 00:13:05.451
You have to realize that
whatever your day is,

00:13:06.792 --> 00:13:09.128
whatever you're doing,
whatever your job is,

00:13:09.128 --> 00:13:13.295
whatever people think of
you or project you to be,

00:13:14.641 --> 00:13:18.688
none of it matters, really,
until you look round,

00:13:18.688 --> 00:13:21.271
and see people who are in need.

00:13:24.141 --> 00:13:28.308
And I think probably the best
benefit of my job is to help.

00:13:33.383 --> 00:13:37.352
To make people smile, who
otherwise wouldn't or couldn't

00:13:37.352 --> 00:13:38.845
have a smile on their face.

00:13:38.845 --> 00:13:40.845
So yeah, that's my idea.

00:13:43.164 --> 00:13:46.747
- I saw Crazy Heart,
and I saw Jeff Bridges

00:13:49.356 --> 00:13:52.926
who is obviously a
brilliant brilliant actor,

00:13:52.926 --> 00:13:54.969
and is capable of so much.

00:13:54.969 --> 00:13:58.636
But what I saw of Jeff
Bridges was so brave,

00:14:00.806 --> 00:14:04.973
and so and the only way that
one can arrive at that arena,

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:10.668
is because you have a
director who's brave.

00:14:10.668 --> 00:14:14.668
And is open to, like I
said, the idea of chance.

00:14:19.349 --> 00:14:23.373
And Scott and I talked
about working together,

00:14:23.373 --> 00:14:25.839
I was desperate to work with the guy,

00:14:25.839 --> 00:14:29.339
and beyond anything I've ever experienced,

00:14:32.679 --> 00:14:36.660
I've never experienced
a director like this.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:40.011
He doesn't allow the
audience to feel the camera,

00:14:40.011 --> 00:14:42.873
to notice the camera, he
makes the camera breath,

00:14:42.873 --> 00:14:45.040
as if it's another person.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.898
You never really even notice
these fancy kind of shots.

00:14:49.898 --> 00:14:53.378
He would do huge, huge
scenes with a lot of people

00:14:53.378 --> 00:14:57.025
in the water, and it was
so freeing, so magnificent

00:14:57.025 --> 00:15:00.460
to play that, because you have
this extended period of time

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:02.188
where it's almost like you've got

00:15:02.188 --> 00:15:04.916
seven minutes of play almost.

00:15:04.916 --> 00:15:08.421
It blew me away. I'm
thinking, "This is this guy's

00:15:08.421 --> 00:15:12.588
"third film. It's his third
film. And he's a master,

00:15:15.298 --> 00:15:17.048
"like he's a master."

00:15:24.923 --> 00:15:28.256
Truly, and if phone books still existed,

00:15:31.068 --> 00:15:34.485
I would film the phone book with the man.

00:15:36.959 --> 00:15:38.035
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:38.035 --> 00:15:40.266
I hope you enjoyed. I
made this video because

00:15:40.266 --> 00:15:43.309
wholistic_Nagef asked me
to, so if there's a famous

00:15:43.309 --> 00:15:45.668
entrepreneur you want me to profile next,

00:15:45.668 --> 00:15:47.743
leave in the comments below,
and I'll see what I can do.

00:15:47.743 --> 00:15:50.040
And also really love to know
what did Johnny say that

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.259
really left a mark on you,

00:15:52.259 --> 00:15:54.358
what was the biggest lesson
that you took from this

00:15:54.358 --> 00:15:57.556
that you will immediately
apply to your life or business?

00:15:57.556 --> 00:15:58.944
Leave it in the comments,

00:15:58.944 --> 00:16:00.035
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:16:00.035 --> 00:16:01.775
Finally, I wanted to give a quick

00:16:01.775 --> 00:16:02.714
shout out to Jennifer Price.

00:16:02.714 --> 00:16:05.542
Jennifer, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:16:05.542 --> 00:16:09.105
and posting it on Facebook, I
really really appreciate it.

00:16:09.105 --> 00:16:10.533
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:16:10.533 --> 00:16:11.694
I believe in you,

00:16:11.694 --> 00:16:14.238
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:16:14.238 --> 00:16:17.421
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:16:17.421 --> 00:16:18.921
I'll see you soon.

00:16:28.812 --> 00:16:31.229
(fast music)

00:16:41.479 --> 00:16:44.421
- This is either Madness or brilliant.

00:16:44.421 --> 00:16:48.060
- It's remarkable how much
those two tend to coincide.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:52.227
There's crazy, there's
insanity, there's delirium,

00:16:54.997 --> 00:16:59.164
there's, I'm mean, but it's,
to me, it's only madness

00:17:01.055 --> 00:17:03.972
and maybe a touch of insanity that,

00:17:06.887 --> 00:17:08.554
that one can access.

00:17:09.594 --> 00:17:13.094
And, to be aware of it growing up doesn't,

00:17:20.422 --> 00:17:22.851
it didn't feel particularly great.

00:17:22.851 --> 00:17:27.018
But, I think it's led me
somehow, to where I am today.

00:17:29.862 --> 00:17:33.112
I'm not saying that I'm completely mad,

00:17:36.529 --> 00:17:38.733
although, I've been accused of that

00:17:38.733 --> 00:17:40.587
many times over the years,

00:17:40.587 --> 00:17:44.170
but, I don't know. I
hope that people will,

00:17:45.248 --> 00:17:47.281
if they do have that tendency,

00:17:47.281 --> 00:17:50.557
that they can find a way to embrace it,

00:17:50.557 --> 00:17:53.474
and use it to their, not advantage,

00:17:56.222 --> 00:17:59.722
let's say, in a, even in a creative sense.

00:18:01.571 --> 00:18:03.390
Use it in a creative sense if you can,

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.890
but, use that to find your way

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:12.147
to be able to stand on solid ground.

00:18:12.147 --> 00:18:15.730
- You've never seen
any of your films ever.

00:18:17.056 --> 00:18:19.223
- I tried to see one once.

00:18:20.767 --> 00:18:22.978
Because the director asked me to.

00:18:22.978 --> 00:18:25.452
My wife, Amber, can
attest to this because her

00:18:25.452 --> 00:18:28.429
elbow was very busy that night.

00:18:28.429 --> 00:18:30.670
And I was sitting next to her,

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:33.471
and the Director's wife is
here, the Director's here,

00:18:33.471 --> 00:18:35.259
I fell asleep 35 times.

00:18:35.259 --> 00:18:37.588
(audience laughs)

00:18:37.588 --> 00:18:38.838
And I got this.

00:18:39.741 --> 00:18:40.574
"Wake up."

00:18:41.656 --> 00:18:44.229
- But part of the reason
is being in the moment,

00:18:44.229 --> 00:18:46.830
when you're acting, and
after, you don't want to...

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:50.997
- I feel the experience.
I'm happy with the process,

00:18:52.729 --> 00:18:54.686
is what I'm interested in.

00:18:54.686 --> 00:18:57.244
The process of creating.

00:18:57.244 --> 00:18:59.327
The process of exploring.

00:19:01.525 --> 00:19:04.138
The process of breaking formula.

00:19:04.138 --> 00:19:08.294
The process of dropping
the bottom out of a scene,

00:19:08.294 --> 00:19:10.711
because people can get stale.

00:19:15.539 --> 00:19:19.166
And I feel that's the wrong place to be.

00:19:19.166 --> 00:19:23.718
So, I'm interested in, and
always have been interested in,

00:19:23.718 --> 00:19:26.551
trying to avoid what was expected.

00:19:29.224 --> 00:19:33.327
And Edward Scissorhands was
certainly the first real

00:19:33.327 --> 00:19:35.558
opportunity for me to feel like I

00:19:35.558 --> 00:19:37.891
was standing on firm ground.

00:19:40.422 --> 00:19:43.101
Cry Baby was important.

00:19:43.101 --> 00:19:46.908
Edward Scissorhands was the
first time that I felt Okay.

00:19:46.908 --> 00:19:51.075
I'm solid. I can start hacking
through, paving my way.

